  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para> Consider the functions \(f(x)=2x\) and \(g(x)=(1.01)^x\).</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Complete the table of values for the functions \(f\) and \(g\).</para>
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(f(x)\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(g(x)\)
         </emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            50
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            500
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(f(x)\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(g(x)\)
         </emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            1.01
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry>
            20
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~1.10
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            50
          </entry>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~1.645
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry>
            200
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~2.7
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            500
          </entry>
          <entry>
            1,000
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~144.8
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Based on the table of values, which function do you think grows faster? Be prepared to show your reasoning.
    </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(f\) is growing faster for the values of \(x\) in the table, but
    as \(x\) increases, \(g\) starts to grow faster. So
    as \(x\) increases, \(g\) may eventually grow faster than \(f\).</para>

</solution></exercise>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Which function do you think will reach a value of 2,000 first? Show your reasoning. If you get stuck, consider
        increasing \(x\) by 100 a few times and record the function values in the table.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>When \(x\) is 600, \(f(600)=1,200\) and \(g(600) \approx 392\), so \(f\) takes a larger value.
      When \(x\) is 700, \(f(700)=1,400\) and \(g(700) \approx 1,059\), so \(f\) still takes a larger value.
      But when \(x\) is 800, \(f(800)=1,600\) and \(g(800) \approx 2,865\), so \(g\) takes a larger value.
  </para>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<section>
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
